\chapter{Paper Enrichment}
\label{enrich-module}

Abstracts are essential for systematic review screening---you cannot evaluate
a paper's relevance from title alone.
Yet not all databases provide them.
DBLP, despite its excellent computer science coverage, returns only
bibliographic metadata.
Web of Science's Starter API omits abstracts entirely.
A search returning hundreds of papers without abstracts is nearly useless for
review purposes.
Similarly, PDF URLs are often missing, making it harder to access full texts.

This chapter implements \emph{paper enrichment}: using DOIs to fetch missing
metadata from providers with better coverage.
The key insight is that the same paper often appears in multiple databases.
A paper found in DBLP likely exists in Semantic Scholar or OpenAlex, where we
can retrieve its abstract and PDF URL.
The DOI serves as the universal identifier linking these representations.

Enrichment operates at two levels:
\begin{description}
\item[Single-paper enrichment] Fetch missing fields on demand, triggered when
  a user views a paper's details in the TUI.
  This provides immediate feedback without pre-processing all results.
\item[Batch enrichment] Enrich all papers in a search session, useful before
  LLM-assisted review where every paper needs an abstract for classification.
\end{description}

We prioritize Semantic Scholar (good abstract coverage, fast API) with
OpenAlex as fallback (broader coverage, always free).

